00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:02.110
So let's review what we've
been talking in the last two

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:03.470
lectures.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.070
We were solving the
Boltzmann question

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.160
looking at the classic
or size effects

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.770
when the heat carriers say,
collide with the boundary.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.220
So we took the simplest
configuration, a thin film

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.470
that has two surfaces.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.790
And two lectures
ago, we discussed

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.720
when you have a heat
flow along the film plan,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.990
where of course, the heat
carrier not just a parallel

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:32.890
flow.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.150
Say the random velocity
can go hit the boundary.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.000
At the boundary, they
diffuse, scatter.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.445
Or the partially diffuse
and partially scatter.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.460
And the second the
problem that we

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:52.210
dealt with in the
last lecture is let's

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:58.090
now have the heat transfer in
the perpendicular direction.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.220
When I say heat, I
hope now you agree,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.530
it's to say I can do charge.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.630
I can do say, photon.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.840
I can do say, molecule.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.040
And in fact that the
molecular heat conduction

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.630
is a more difficult
problem, believe it or not,

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.440
that's because if I
do the gas molecule,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.560
I have also a
concentration gradient.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:28.270
So even the same material,
the higher temperature,

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:31.270
if you think about ideal
gas law, and the density

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.390
will change.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.848
So when density change, you
also have a mass diffusion.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:37.390
In other words, in
the same material,

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.790
there is a mass diffusion.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.930
That's an integral equation.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.010
So you actually have to solve
two integral equations together

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.200
for the molecular case.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.470
For fallout, let's just say
it's not considered the density

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.130
variations.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.420
So it's one integral equation.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.120
And the difference
is in this case,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.480
the temperature is
inside the integral.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.390
So even we use the relaxation
time approximation,

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.690
the Boltzmann equation
itself, is the first order

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.000
differentiable
equation by the end

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.510
because transport
is a long local.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:21.279
When I say long local, I mean,
the heat flow at any point

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:23.320
is not just a local gradient.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.850
It depends on the temperature
history directly integration

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.360
through the whole domain.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.270
So that's the
non-local transport.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.210
And then we have to solve
the integral equation.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:42.160
And the rarefied gas flow,
we didn't solve this problem.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.600
But I discussed if
you go to solve,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.930
you start off
combination of the two.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.680
Because I have flow
in the x-direction,

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.360
I have a source term that's
a function of x But also, I

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.470
have the boundary that
constrain the molecules

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.270
and the boundaries scatter.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.610
So if we solve this, I
discussed the [? Lucien ?]

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.520
minimum phenomena.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.510
And the velocity
distribution now has a slip.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:18.490
So there is a discontinuity
of the molecules, either wall.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:23.960
And I want to say that
this discontinuity--

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.160
so normally if you
say, in 2005, what

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.520
were undergraduate fluid
mechanics heat transfer,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.780
we always say at the wall,
the temperature is continuous.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.960
The velocity equals
the wall velocity.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.780
That's the long-sleeve boundary
condition for both temperature

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.590
and for velocity.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:48.190
And the reality is
there is always slip.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:52.570
That slip happens over 1
molecular mean free path.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:55.570
So now let's think
about the air molecule

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:58.300
free path, 100 nanometer.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.030
And if I have slip
over 100 nanometer,

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.700
but if I deal with
the meter scale,

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:08.740
so that slip is too small
to be actually observable

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.090
or of any significance.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:17.890
So this is always happening
except for in the case

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:18.790
of liquid.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:23.690
I will move on to
the liquid version.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:29.210
So that's what we deal
with, more or less,

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.550
in the rigorous in terms
of solving the Boltzmann

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.810
equation on the relaxation
time approximation.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.150
And of course, the
difficulty is now

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:42.290
even for this simplest
problem like heat conduction,

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.810
you realize that we have to
go solve an integral equation,

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:49.580
which is by no means, trivial.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:56.360
So what I'm going to introduce
relaxed some approximate ways,

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:00.440
just attribute all the
effects to the boundary,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:01.830
to the interface.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.340
So we'll use diffusion
approximation,

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.820
using for real or in
the inside the domain.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.180
But at the boundary, I gave
a slip boundary condition.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:17.070
So how I should derive that
slip boundary condition?

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.910
And I want to actually
say I want to show you

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:21.600
a few papers I wrote on this.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:28.190
And it turns out every field has
this slip boundary condition.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:31.700
In the radiation, there's the
so-called [? Dessler ?] jump,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:33.410
boundary condition.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:36.440
And in fluid mechanics,
there's a first order slip,

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.060
second order slip.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.030
And in heat
conduction, I mentioned

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.190
there's a temperature
jump when we solve

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:46.310
say, the perpendicular flow.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:54.030
And in electrical field, there
is also interfacial resistance,

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:55.530
electrical resistance.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.365
So this is all related
to what is this slip.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:11.700
I'll make more comments when
we go to look at these papers.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:23.970
But the diffusion approximation
plus boundary slip--

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.250
so what I'm saying
is the diffusion

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:34.990
approximation, of
course, is what

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.909
we've been talking chapter 6.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:41.260
That's how we derive Fourier
law, Ohm's law, and all

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.190
these continuum equations.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:51.800
And what I want is to use
this diffusion approximation.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:56.590
So the way I derive their part
diffusion is the distribution

00:08:56.590 --> 00:09:05.650
function, f equals f0
minus tau vx, df0 dx.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.950
So I'm not writing
the fourth term.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:12.040
For electron, you have
to write the fourth term.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:17.020
So this is the one that I we
use to derive the Fourier law--

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:19.435
df0/dx df0 dt/dx.

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:24.550
So what I'm going
to do is the idea

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:29.290
is that I'm going to use this,
assume this small deviation

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.570
from equilibrium, and
this distribution function

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:37.130
