that you would like to be.

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:18.510
And what I'm going
to do is to give you

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:22.860
a very brief introduction,
what's the mathematical tool

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:24.240
to do it.

00:53:24.240 --> 00:53:26.700
It's still based on
Boltzmann equation.

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:29.070
But now let's look at
the interaction, focus

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:30.760
on the interaction term.

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:36.660
So if you recall, we talk about
briefly, the Fermi Golden rule.

00:53:46.830 --> 00:53:51.090
What it say is that if
I have original system,

00:53:51.090 --> 00:53:53.670
I have this
eigenstates that I got

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:56.760
from quantum
mechanical solutions.

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:58.680
And now I have a perturbation.

00:53:58.680 --> 00:54:01.620
I have a photon comes
in, for example.

00:54:01.620 --> 00:54:07.540
And that creates a perturbation
to the energy of the system.

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:12.510
A Hamiltonian, that's
a pretty big operation

00:54:12.510 --> 00:54:17.130
on top of already,
the eigenstates.

00:54:17.130 --> 00:54:20.940
So the Fermi Golden rule is
this is the initial stage,

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:23.880
this is the final stage,
that's the original solution,

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:25.590
allowable solution.

00:54:25.590 --> 00:54:28.710
And then the Fermi
Golden rule tells me

00:54:28.710 --> 00:54:37.400
the rate of that process
is 2 pi over H-bar.

00:54:37.400 --> 00:54:43.200
And m, initial to
final scattering matrix

00:54:43.200 --> 00:54:46.170
delta of the energy,
the final state energy

00:54:46.170 --> 00:54:48.563
minus initial state energy.

00:54:48.563 --> 00:54:49.980
That's what the
Fermi Golden rule.

00:54:49.980 --> 00:54:54.210
And the scatter
matrix is related

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:57.750
to the wave function
of the final

00:54:57.750 --> 00:55:02.100
and the initial states and
the perturbation Hamiltonian.

00:55:05.980 --> 00:55:14.710
So we have the scatter matrix
equals the final state wave

00:55:14.710 --> 00:55:19.300
function, the interaction
perturbation Hamiltonian,

00:55:19.300 --> 00:55:25.240
and the initial state
wave function integrated

00:55:25.240 --> 00:55:27.822
over the volume of
the interaction.

00:55:30.400 --> 00:55:33.410
So what I want to
give is one example.

00:55:33.410 --> 00:55:35.950
This kind of
scattering calculation

00:55:35.950 --> 00:55:38.260
is extremely involved.

00:55:38.260 --> 00:55:40.930
And say I want to use
one example to say people

00:55:40.930 --> 00:55:42.620
have done a lot of work.

00:55:42.620 --> 00:55:46.690
And if you want to look at it,
you should check literature.

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:50.410
And this is one example, let's
look at the electron photon

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:51.462
scattering.

00:56:01.850 --> 00:56:07.280
So if you think
about how electrons

00:56:07.280 --> 00:56:11.270
will go through
semiconductor, how

00:56:11.270 --> 00:56:15.730
electrons get
scattered by photons.

00:56:15.730 --> 00:56:20.080
And any process, you have to--

00:56:20.080 --> 00:56:22.660
I think I made those
comments before physics

00:56:22.660 --> 00:56:24.760
and even reciprocal space.

00:56:24.760 --> 00:56:28.930
But also, you have to go
think about the real event.

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:31.720
If you have a real world
picture, real space

00:56:31.720 --> 00:56:34.380
picture of what's
happening, then you

00:56:34.380 --> 00:56:37.170
can describe that in terms
of the reciprocal space

00:56:37.170 --> 00:56:40.440
is just math, mathematics.

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:47.700
And if I think about electron,
my picture before is--

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:49.560
if you think about
crowning a penny model,

00:56:49.560 --> 00:56:51.480
I have periodical potential.

00:56:51.480 --> 00:56:56.030
That solution will give
me a bandgap, for example.

00:56:56.030 --> 00:57:04.390
And of course, when I did with
the photon, I have a spring.

00:57:04.390 --> 00:57:13.870
So the bandgap for this let's
say, one-dimensional lattice,

00:57:13.870 --> 00:57:21.270
depends on, of course,
the lattice constant

00:57:21.270 --> 00:57:25.030
and depends on the
potential profile.

00:57:25.030 --> 00:57:30.880
Now what photon does is
when your lattices vibrate,

00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:32.335
the lattice constant
is changing.

00:57:34.920 --> 00:57:40.040
The lattice constant change,
you could say this fluctuation,

00:57:40.040 --> 00:57:41.970
visually you can
think about if my band

00:57:41.970 --> 00:57:45.630
gap depends on lattice constant,
lattice consonant is vibrating.

00:57:45.630 --> 00:57:49.880
So the electron bandgap
is changing there

00:57:49.880 --> 00:57:53.280
due to the lattice vibration.

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:55.260
And that created
the perturbation

00:57:55.260 --> 00:57:57.960
for the electron energy
level and created

00:57:57.960 --> 00:58:00.210
a scatter of the electron.

00:58:00.210 --> 00:58:02.290
So let's try this problem.

00:58:02.290 --> 00:58:08.070
And so the perturbation
to the Hamiltonian--

00:58:08.070 --> 00:58:10.740
and in the 1D,
let's say electron

00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:14.070
is close to the bandgap is
a function of the lattice

00:58:14.070 --> 00:58:22.920
constant and the displacement
of each lattice point.

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:26.070
So this gives you
a delta a the mass

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:29.110
of the initial steady state.

00:58:29.110 --> 00:58:32.610
So this is the in the 1D.

00:58:32.610 --> 00:58:41.400
If I do a 3D situation,
I can do an expansion.

00:58:41.400 --> 00:58:52.650
So if I do Taylor expansion,
it's dEc/da du/dx x times a.

00:58:52.650 --> 00:59:02.880
So in 3D, this can be written
as equals Za and the divergence

00:59:02.880 --> 00:59:06.030
of the atomic
displacement vector, u.

00:59:10.330 --> 00:59:17.050
And this Za is the called
the deformation potential.

00:59:17.050 --> 00:59:30.580
Za is how the bandgap check
the conduction band will change

00:59:30.580 --> 00:59:36.030
as a function of the volume
change of the crystal,

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:40.920
so if you squeeze the crystal,
how the bandgap would change.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:46.560
So this Za is called
deformation potential.

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:54.310
And now I'm going to use this
perturbed Hamiltonian back

00:59:54.310 --> 01:00:00.250
into the Fermi
Golden rule and try

01:00:00.250 --> 01:00:05.423
to say what is this
scatter matrix from i to f.

01:00:08.670 --> 01:00:11.400
So this is the math part.

01:00:11.400 --> 01:00:16.650
And the way to do
it is you say you

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:20.610
are going to integrate over the
volume, your initial function

01:00:20.610 --> 01:00:24.030
and your final wave function.

01:00:24.030 --> 01:00:28.680
And those are the wave functions
of the electron because I'm

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:32.430
looking at the electron gets
scattered by lattice vibration.

01:00:32.430 --> 01:00:38.100
So the lattice vibration is
perturbed, the Hamiltonian

01:00:38.100 --> 01:00:39.090
of the electron.

01:00:39.090 --> 01:00:43.150
So physically,
what we have is you

01:00:43.150 --> 01:00:49.980
can have a electron at a certain
wave vector energy come in

01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:52.800
and interact with
a lattice wave.

01:00:52.800 --> 01:00:58.260
And let's say at a certain wave
vector and a certain frequency.

01:00:58.260 --> 01:01:04.110
And this can be combined.

01:01:04.110 --> 01:01:08.520
The energy of the photon
is given to the electron.

01:01:08.520 --> 01:01:15.150
So you gather the momentum,
energy, new momentum,

01:01:15.150 --> 01:01:16.650
new energy for electron.

01:01:16.650 --> 01:01:18.390
This is the inelastic process.

01:01:18.390 --> 01:01:21.010
The photon disappear.

01:01:21.010 --> 01:01:23.400
You could also have
the process where

01:01:23.400 --> 01:01:26.770
electron with a certain
energy, momentum

01:01:26.770 --> 01:01:29.590
is split it into another
electron at a different energy

01:01:29.590 --> 01:01:32.620
momentum with a photon,
create a photon.

01:01:32.620 --> 01:01:35.740
So that's the
interaction process.

01:01:35.740 --> 01:01:39.400
And mathematically
what it will say

01:01:39.400 --> 01:01:45.280
is I want to plug this
into the scatter matrix.

01:01:45.280 --> 01:01:55.270
But the wave function, if
I know the wave vector,

01:01:55.270 --> 01:01:57.700
I can use the prime
wave representation.

01:02:01.460 --> 01:02:05.000
The wave function is a
proportional exponential, i, k,

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:07.280
r.

01:02:07.280 --> 01:02:11.760
You have an energy
frequency also in there.

01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:15.950
But the energy part
is actually taken

01:02:15.950 --> 01:02:21.740
care of by this delta function
in the Fermi Golden rule.

01:02:21.740 --> 01:02:23.720
You could keep, that
everything together.

01:02:23.720 --> 01:02:26.825
You'll see the latter coming
out of this delta function.

01:02:30.240 --> 01:02:37.830
If I look at the displacement
of the atom, the displacement

01:02:37.830 --> 01:02:45.180
of the atom, again, now you
can go say this is each space,

01:02:45.180 --> 01:02:48.570
each point in the crystal could
have a different displacement.

01:02:48.570 --> 01:02:51.210
But we did a Fourier
transform before.

01:02:51.210 --> 01:02:54.930
Any function, you can express
in terms of sine, and cosine.

01:02:54.930 --> 01:02:56.550
That's the Fourier series.

01:02:56.550 --> 01:03:01.580
This is the reciprocal
lattice representation.

01:03:01.580 --> 01:03:07.050
So we can say different
wave vector of the u, q,

01:03:07.050 --> 01:03:10.080
and exponential mass, i.

01:03:10.080 --> 01:03:14.530
We've got t minus q dot r.

01:03:14.530 --> 01:03:20.280
Eventually this omega t will
be merged into the energy,

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:23.280
into part of the
energy conservation.

01:03:23.280 --> 01:03:27.210
But I wrote it down
here because to say,

01:03:27.210 --> 01:03:30.690
when we think about the energy
of the lattice vibration,

01:03:30.690 --> 01:03:33.930
we need to say 1/2 mv square.

01:03:33.930 --> 01:03:37.500
That time derivative
will give me omega.

01:03:41.800 --> 01:03:47.200
Now if I go to plug in
these two wave functions

01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:57.680
into the scatter matrix,
the scatter matrix from k

01:03:57.680 --> 01:04:03.240
to k prime, this is the electron
scattering matrix with vector.

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:08.690
That means say I have one is a
k, the either initial or final.

01:04:08.690 --> 01:04:11.900
The other is a k-prime
initial or final.

01:04:11.900 --> 01:04:20.270
And what I have then is this one
could be written as Za over v.

01:04:20.270 --> 01:04:29.600
And because I'm substituting
in the Za, the Hamiltonian,

01:04:29.600 --> 01:04:32.630
and then I have when
I substitute the u,

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:36.350
this H-prime into
the expression,

01:04:36.350 --> 01:04:50.916
I will have a qu q times the
uq and exponential mass i

01:04:50.916 --> 01:04:56.810
k-prime plus q minus k times r.

01:05:01.590 --> 01:05:05.730
You see here, I didn't carry
that mass, only the t term,

01:05:05.730 --> 01:05:10.180
just looking at this
special term here.

01:05:10.180 --> 01:05:14.350
So the point I want
to make is here

01:05:14.350 --> 01:05:17.710
if you look at this expression,
what really scatter photon?

01:05:17.710 --> 01:05:22.390
Uq, so for photon,
this is the plane wave.

01:05:22.390 --> 01:05:25.480
This is a photon with vector,
as a wave vector direction,

01:05:25.480 --> 01:05:28.060
photon wave
propagation direction.

01:05:28.060 --> 01:05:31.060
This is the lattice
displacement direction.

01:05:31.060 --> 01:05:34.960
Whether it's a transverse wave,
if it's a perpendicular to q,

01:05:34.960 --> 01:05:37.470
or it's a longitudinal
wave, if it's

01:05:37.470 --> 01:05:38.851
in the same direction of q.

01:05:41.500 --> 01:05:45.550
Because of this deformation
potential, at the end

01:05:45.550 --> 01:05:51.504
my scatter matrix is the
dot product of q times u.

01:05:51.504 --> 01:05:56.000
It immediately tells you
that transverse photon here

01:05:56.000 --> 01:06:01.790
do not scatter electrons,
because this is 0.

01:06:01.790 --> 01:06:06.770
And it's a longitudinal photon
that will scatter electrons.

01:06:06.770 --> 01:06:08.750
That's one.

01:06:08.750 --> 01:06:13.880
Secondly, if we look at
this integration, plane wave

01:06:13.880 --> 01:06:19.010
integration, this will be
rapidly sine cosine function

01:06:19.010 --> 01:06:22.370
average over the volume.

01:06:22.370 --> 01:06:24.260
Because this is exponential.

01:06:24.260 --> 01:06:28.250
And it's a complex function,
is either sine and cosine.

01:06:28.250 --> 01:06:31.055
If you average sine and
cosine over a large number,

01:06:31.055 --> 01:06:33.990
you know it's 0.

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:40.950
So unless those exponents is 0,
the rest of this is always 0.

01:06:40.950 --> 01:06:45.000
So what you have here,
essentially with this mass,

01:06:45.000 --> 01:06:47.070
you gather the
momentum conservation.

01:06:47.070 --> 01:06:53.490
Because when this one is 0
it's 2 pi of reciprocal lattice

01:06:53.490 --> 01:06:58.090
factor, you get this one
will not be a 0 function.

01:06:58.090 --> 01:07:06.210
So what you have is a
delta k-prime plus q

01:07:06.210 --> 01:07:09.210
because I have this and the k.

01:07:09.210 --> 01:07:16.050
So what it means is that,
k-prime plus q minus k

01:07:16.050 --> 01:07:17.340
equals 0.

01:07:17.340 --> 01:07:20.550
That's the momentum
conservation.

01:07:20.550 --> 01:07:22.830
Hk is the electron momentum.

01:07:22.830 --> 01:07:25.850
Hq is the photon momentum.

01:07:25.850 --> 01:07:29.180
So it came from laterally,
from this integration.

01:07:29.180 --> 01:07:33.160
And in fact, if this
one is rather not

01:07:33.160 --> 01:07:36.640
0, is equal to g-- g is
reciprocal lattice factor,

01:07:36.640 --> 01:07:39.340
we talked before.

01:07:39.340 --> 01:07:41.120
And that's still fine.

01:07:41.120 --> 01:07:42.760
You integrate that.

01:07:42.760 --> 01:07:46.720
It will not be 0 because
you got a 2 pi, i 2 pi.

01:07:46.720 --> 01:07:52.610
And but say, for electron
photon, this seldom happens.

01:07:52.610 --> 01:07:56.570
It's typically a
Patagonian semiconductor.

01:07:56.570 --> 01:08:02.000
Because the semiconductor
is a conduction band.

01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:06.400
And most of the electron
is at this location.

01:08:06.400 --> 01:08:07.915
So it doesn't need to--

01:08:10.510 --> 01:08:13.270
with g, it's the
reciprocal lattice vector,

01:08:13.270 --> 01:08:16.850
pi over a, that's a very
large momentum there.

01:08:16.850 --> 01:08:19.939
So that the rate of that
process don't happen often.

01:08:19.939 --> 01:08:21.469
It's a typical normal process.

01:08:25.620 --> 01:08:33.149
So essentially, you can
also relate the qu velocity,

01:08:33.149 --> 01:08:39.090
the amplitude of all
the lattice displacement

01:08:39.090 --> 01:08:43.590
to how many photon you have.

01:08:43.590 --> 01:08:45.899
Because this is all
related to energy.

01:08:45.899 --> 01:08:49.380
One is the photon picture.

01:08:49.380 --> 01:08:53.560
Displacement is the
actual moment there.

01:08:53.560 --> 01:08:59.670
So if you think
about the energy,

01:08:59.670 --> 01:09:02.117
because I want to relate
this to temperature.

01:09:05.600 --> 01:09:14.020
So energy conservation, what
we have is 1/2 mv squared.

01:09:14.020 --> 01:09:17.380
This is the kinetic energy.

01:09:17.380 --> 01:09:21.950
Plus potential energy,
1/2 kx squared,

01:09:21.950 --> 01:09:26.210
that's equal to the total
energy of the photons.

01:09:26.210 --> 01:09:31.640
So if I do that, what I'll
have is 1/2 half of v,

01:09:31.640 --> 01:09:35.450
omega squared and
the uq squared.

01:09:35.450 --> 01:09:38.580
So this is a kinetic
plus potential energy

01:09:38.580 --> 01:09:42.680
equals h-bar omega nq.

01:09:42.680 --> 01:09:49.000
Nq is the number of
photons at that temperature

01:09:49.000 --> 01:09:51.270
with this momentum.

01:09:51.270 --> 01:09:55.380
At equilibrium, that's
the Boltzmann state,

01:09:55.380 --> 01:10:00.610
no, the Bose-Einstein
statistics.

01:10:00.610 --> 01:10:06.770
So this one, nq, is a
function of temperature omega

01:10:06.770 --> 01:10:07.990
at equilibrium.

01:10:07.990 --> 01:10:11.020
So the reason I do
this is so that I

01:10:11.020 --> 01:10:13.060
can write the final
expression in terms

01:10:13.060 --> 01:10:15.940
of different temperature,
what's the electron-photon

01:10:15.940 --> 01:10:18.780
interaction?

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:22.500
I convert this
displacement factor

01:10:22.500 --> 01:10:27.460
in terms of the
number of photons.

01:10:27.460 --> 01:10:31.050
So if you do this, you go
to say essentially, write

01:10:31.050 --> 01:10:34.150
the scattering term, df/dt.

01:10:37.000 --> 01:10:42.970
And this term, let's say
let's look at this first one.

01:10:42.970 --> 01:10:48.890
We look at the this term.

01:10:48.890 --> 01:10:55.930
So from k-prime absorb a
photon, and in lation process,

01:10:55.930 --> 01:10:58.780
go to another electron.

01:10:58.780 --> 01:11:08.150
And for this process, when we
sum up, this is the transfer,

01:11:08.150 --> 01:11:10.120
this is a 1.

01:11:10.120 --> 01:11:14.110
From k to k-prime
or k-prime to k,

01:11:14.110 --> 01:11:18.140
you can write different
states, slight different forms.

01:11:18.140 --> 01:11:24.970
So what I have here is
this is the sum of all q

01:11:24.970 --> 01:11:33.200
and 2 pi h-bar in the
front and the q squared,

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:45.740
and the h Za squared and
2 rho v omega and the nq.

01:11:45.740 --> 01:11:48.950
So this is the scatter matrix.

01:11:48.950 --> 01:11:53.270
Fermi Golden rule, I'm
applying Fermi Golden rule,

01:11:53.270 --> 01:11:57.080
2 pi h square.

01:11:57.080 --> 01:12:01.850
And then now when
I scatter, I say,

01:12:01.850 --> 01:12:05.480
from how many electrons I
have, either the k-prime--

01:12:05.480 --> 01:12:10.700
because this will go
in from k-prime to k.

01:12:10.700 --> 01:12:15.990
So that's the Fermi
Dirac statistics I have,

01:12:15.990 --> 01:12:19.680
how many electrons I
have at this k-prime.

01:12:19.680 --> 01:12:22.500
But then when I
scatter into k, I

01:12:22.500 --> 01:12:24.760
have to see how many
states are empty.

01:12:24.760 --> 01:12:28.860
This is the Pauli
exclusion principle.

01:12:28.860 --> 01:12:30.570
So this is a k.

01:12:33.090 --> 01:12:36.000
Then I have with
this delta function,

01:12:36.000 --> 01:12:45.960
k-prime q to k, that's a result
out of my scatter matrix.

01:12:45.960 --> 01:12:48.780
And then I have my
energy conservation,

01:12:48.780 --> 01:12:51.840
another delta for E--

01:12:51.840 --> 01:12:54.330
this is the final energy--

01:12:54.330 --> 01:12:57.580
equals the sum of
E-prime plus h omega.

01:12:57.580 --> 01:13:01.020
So that's the
E-prime plus h omega.

01:13:04.520 --> 01:13:09.860
Very complex, but people
actually did this.

01:13:09.860 --> 01:13:12.260
And if you go the
read papers there,

01:13:12.260 --> 01:13:17.000
sometimes people still do it.
electron photon scattering

01:13:17.000 --> 01:13:20.820
is very difficult. And this is
one of the four possibilities.

01:13:20.820 --> 01:13:23.580
So you actually have
four terms like this.

01:13:23.580 --> 01:13:27.260
This term we talked
about is from k-prime

01:13:27.260 --> 01:13:30.250
to k by absorber photon.

01:13:30.250 --> 01:13:34.210
I can have from k-prime
to k to emitter photon.

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:39.400
Both of this processes
increase the number

01:13:39.400 --> 01:13:43.440
